WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.100
Home systems: routines, shared chores, meals and money.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.190
This is the video for Module 3, Home Management Systems.

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:18.610
A routine is a set of tasks done in the same order at roughly the same time. Routines save time because you stop deciding what to do next.

4
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.430
They also make it easier for others in the home to help, because everyone knows what happens when.

5
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:33.930
Most of a calm morning is actually prepared the night before. That is why the best first routine is the evening reset.

6
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:43.310
Even 20 to 30 minutes each evening makes a big difference. Clear the kitchen and wash the pots. Set out uniforms and bags.

7
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:51.220
Check tomorrow's plan, put the phones on charge, and do a 10-minute tidy of the main room with everyone helping.

8
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:58.350
Then spread the big cleaning across the week, one main task a day of 20 to 40 minutes, so Saturday is lighter.

9
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:07.620
When you clean bathrooms and toilets, never mix bleach, such as Hypo, with other cleaning products. Dangerous gases can form.

10
00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:15.270
Open windows, wear gloves, and store all chemicals high up, in their labelled containers, away from children and food.

11
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.570
If one woman carries most of the work, working faster will not fix it. Sharing will.

12
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:30.350
First, list every task the home needs. Include the mental load, like remembering fees deadlines or noticing the gas is low.

13
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:41.090
Second, match tasks to ability. Children can do more than we expect, but keep young children away from hot pots, knives, chemicals and sockets.

14
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:48.450
Third, hold a short family meeting. Let each person choose some tasks first, then post the chart.

15
00:01:48.800 --> 00:02:03.880
For example, Fatima in Ilorin listed 28 tasks. Her son chose dishes and sweeping, her daughter chose setting the table, and her husband took the Saturday market run. Her housework dropped by about 1.5 hours a day.

16
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:15.650
A common mistake is handing over only the doing. Please buy gas when I tell you still leaves the remembering with you. And redoing their work teaches them it does not matter.

17
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:27.390
Instead, hand over the whole task. You are in charge of buying gas: check the level every Friday and buy when it is low. Accept good enough, and thank them specifically.

18
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:37.450
Plan the week's meals from what you already have, write one market list, and cook a big pot of stew that serves several meals. Then keep it safe.

19
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.180
Wash your hands, and use separate boards for raw meat and ready-to-eat food.

20
00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:52.150
Cool cooked food quickly and put it in the fridge or freezer within 2 hours, in covered portions for one meal. Do not leave stew out overnight.

21
00:02:53.400 --> 00:03:03.660
Reheat until steaming hot all the way through, and reheat only once. If frozen food fully thaws in a long power cut, cook it straight away or throw it away.

22
00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:10.860
A budget is a plan for your money before you spend it. Split it into needs, wants and savings.

23
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:17.020
For example, Ruth and her husband in Jos bring in about 400,000 naira a month.

24
00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:31.370
Needs like rent, food, school fees, transport and gas come to 320,000 naira. That is 80%, and in many Nigerian households needs take more than half. That is fine.

25
00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:37.930
They save 40,000 naira, which is 10%, and keep 40,000 for wants.

26
00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.060
Together that is 400,000. Every naira has a job.

27
00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:57.640
Meal planning cut their food spending from about 160,000 to 140,000, and the 20,000 saved goes to the emergency fund. Never share your PIN or OTP with anyone.

28
00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:14.680
To remember. Start with the evening reset. Share whole tasks, not just the doing. Keep batch-cooked food safe. Give every naira a job. And keep one family calendar, starting checklists four to six weeks before busy times like school resumption.

29
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:25.290
Now write your routines, create your chore chart, plan a week of meals and a month of money, and build your family calendar. Continue with the lessons below.
